W:There was a fair maiden who lived all alone,
W:She lived all alone on the shore o,
W:And no one could she find that would calm her sweet mind
W:But to wander alone on the shore, shore, shore,
W:To wander alone on the shore o.
W:
W:There was a brave captain who sailed a fine ship,
W:The weather being steady and fair o.
W:"I shall die, I shall die," this brave captain did cry,
W:"If I can't have this maid on the shore, shore, shore,
W:If I can't have this maid on the shore o."
W:
W:After many persuasions they brought her on board,
W:And the captain sat down her a chair o.
W:He invited her down to his cabin below,
W:Farewell sorrow, farewell now now care o,
W:Farewell sorrow, farewell now now care o.
W:
W:"Well, I'll sing you a song," the fair maiden did cry,
W:And the captain was weeping for joy o.
W:She sang it so sweetly, so soft, so completely,
W:She sang captain and sailors to sleep o,
W:She sang captain and sailors to sleep o.
W:
W:Now she robbed them of wealth, and she robbed them of gold,
W:She robbed them of fine costly fare o.
W:And the captain's broad sword, she used as an oar,
W:And she rowed herself back to the shore, shore, shore,
W:She rowed herself back to the shore o.
W:
W:Now the men, they were mad, yet the men they were sad.
W:They were deeply sunk down in despair o.
W:To see her go 'way with her booty so gay
W:With her rings and her things and her fine fare o
W:Her rings and her things and her fare o.
W:
W:"Now, do not be sad or sunk down in despair,
W:You should have known me before o.
W:I sang you to sleep, and I robbed you of wealth,
W:And again I'm a maid on the shore, shore, shore,
W:Again I'm a maid on the shore o."
